Q: How to have background around text, but white space in between lines? A: <p>You can now do this:</p>
<pre><code>p {
    text-decoration-line: underline;
    text-decoration-color: #sexycolor;
    text-decoration-thickness: 20px;
    text-underline-offset: -10px;
    text-decoration-skip-ink: none;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Skip ink is necessary because this ignores the text itself, and you can freely position the line via offset</p>
<p>and for extra fanciness you can use CSS variable to make the line always match the text size:</p>
<pre><code>text-decoration-line: underline;
text-decoration-color: var(--txtInvertColor);
text-decoration-thickness: calc(var(--font-size-M) + 1rem);
text-underline-offset: calc(var(--font-size-M) * -1);
text-decoration-skip-ink: none;
</code></pre>

Q: How to accept a closure that accepts a closure? A: <p>This is not possible with a closure without using dynamic dispatch, but is possible with a helper trait.</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>trait WithFunction {
    fn with_function(self, f: impl FnOnce());
}
</code></pre>
<p>The only downside is that defining one of these is a bit clunky. You can't use a closure, so every time you want to define one you will need to create a new struct for it.</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>fn call_closure_with_closure&lt;F&gt;(f: F)
where
    F: WithFunction,
{
    let mut called = false;
    f.with_function(|| {
        called = true;
    });
    assert!(called);
}

fn main() {
    struct MyFn;

    impl WithFunction for MyFn {
        fn with_function(f: impl FnOnce()) {
            println!(&quot;before!&quot;);
            f();
            println!(&quot;after!&quot;);
        }
    }

    call_closure_with_closure(MyFn);
}
</code></pre>

Q: Issue compiling template class A: <p>Here is MCVE:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct Base
{
    void foo(){}
};

template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct Derived: Base&lt;T&gt;
{
    void bar()
    {
        foo();
    }
};

int main()
{
    Derived&lt;int&gt;{}.bar();
}
</code></pre>
